<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karaeng Pattingalloang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1600">1600</a>–<a href="/years/1654">1654</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karaeng Pattingalloang (1600–1654) was a nobleman of Tallo and chancellor of the twin kingdom of Gowa–Tallo in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> from 1639. Famous among European visitors for his learning, he read Portuguese and Spanish, collected European books and maps, and ordered a large globe from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. He guided the policy of Makassar at the height of its power as a free trading port.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pattingalloang was the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaeng-matoaya">Karaeng Matoaya</a>, ruler of Tallo and chief minister, or Tuma'bicara-butta, of the partner kingdom of Gowa during its rapid rise to become one of the leading independent ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. He was probably educated in part by the Portuguese, the largest European community in Makassar, for as an adult he spoke Portuguese, in one visitor's words, as fluently as people from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>. He succeeded his father as chief minister and held the office until his death.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>He built up a substantial library of books in Portuguese, Spanish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Malay, and sponsored, if he did not write himself, translations of military manuals into Makassarese. He may have been the first Southeast Asian to grasp the importance of mathematics to European science and warfare. The French Jesuit Alexandre de Rhodes called him exceedingly wise and sensible, despite his adherence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and wrote that he had read the chronicles of the European kings and always had books in hand, above all on mathematics.</p><p>He pressed visiting ships for books and rarities. In 1644 he sent a shipload of sandalwood to Dutch Batavia to pay for a large globe showing the latest discoveries, labelled in Spanish, Portuguese or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rather than Dutch. The leading mapmakers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> took many years to make it; it was among the largest such globes ever built and inspired others for royal households, but it arrived only after his death and his successors cared little for it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In statecraft he steered Gowa between quarrelling Europeans and Muslims, and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> demands for a monopoly he insisted that his port remain open to all. Only after his death did Makassar fall under the combined pressure of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> rebellion and Dutch attack. Even a Dutch adversary called him a man of great knowledge, science and understanding.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karaeng Pattingalloang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Karaeng Pattingalloang</b>, famous for his learning, collected European books, maps and a globe.</blockquote><a href="/state/tallo-kingdom">Tallo Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
